From 73335d458db4e3bb9ba5b57c4fb3b0065e746c25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 04:08:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] irqchip: add Realtek RTL819x interrupt controller

Add an OF irqchip driver for the RTL8197F secondary interrupt
controller: two 32-bit mask/status banks (GIMR/GISR and GIMR2/GISR2)
cascade up to 64 peripheral inputs into the MIPS CPU interrupt
controller, and IRR0-7 route each source to one of the CPU IP2-IP7
lines, the route of the lowest-numbered input in the lowest nibble.

The routing is taken from DT instead of a hard-coded table: mapping an
input writes its IRR nibble with the CPU line of the parent named by
the input's specifier.  Inputs never referenced from DT stay
disconnected - every IRR field is cleared at probe time - instead of
silently reaching IP2 as the vendor defaults would have them; this
also drops the implicit assumption that unlisted bank 1 inputs reach
IP2 through the input 31 aggregator, which contradicted the all-zero
table entries written for them.  Bank 1 is still scanned
unconditionally by every parent handler: input 31 aggregates bank 1,
and whether it covers every unmasked bank 1 source is undocumented, so
relying on it for delivery is not safe.

The SoC dtsi routes the generic sources plus TIMER0 (input 7, the
system tick source of the RTL819x timer driver) on IP2, the switch
core on IP4, the PCIe root port on IP5, the WLAN MAC on IP6 and the
CPU timer on IP7; the WLAN MAC and CPU timer have no in-tree consumer
yet and stay routed but idle until a driver claims them.  The core
never asserts the CP0 Compare interrupt on an IP line itself (it only
raises Cause.TI); the SoC controller observes it as input 47 ("CPU SI
timer"), which drives the r4k clock event device.

The bank count (2 on the RTL8197F, 1 on the single-bank RTL8196E,
RTL8197D and RTL8881A) is taken from match data, so the driver covers
the whole family; only the RTL8197F compatible is declared for now.
Input 31 of the first bank aggregates the second bank and cannot be
mapped.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
